  "notes": "British Eurodance-pop with line-dance choreography, and the choreography is the arrangement fact the entry makes explicit: \"the choreography means 8-bar sections and a fixed instrumental break for the dance routine\". Every section length here is therefore 8 bars and every structure carries an instrumental section (written as `bridge`, the nearest legal section name) between the choruses. The vocal parameter is the other identity — \"a five-voice group arrangement — solo lines traded in verses, full unison-plus-harmony in choruses, with a male/female call-and-response layer\" — so the melody is call-and-response and the countermelody sits an octave below at a 0.7 density, the answering half of that trade. Harmony is bright majors with \"a I-V-vi-IV or IV-V-I cadence and a key-raising final chorus; minor keys reserved for the ballads\", marked `[LOW-CONF]` in the research and repeated here. The kit is kick on all four, layered clap, offbeat open hats and a 16th tambourine. ⚠ The INFERRED parameters are the velocities, registers and densities; the research calls the parameters genre-derived.",
